/* Generated by scripts/build-essay-course-catalog.mjs from public/audio/essay-course/*.txt. Do not edit by hand. */
export const essayCourseNarration: Record<string, { audio: string; script: string }> = {
  "answer-the-question": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/answer-the-question.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson one. Answer the question that is set.\n\nWelcome to your essay course. I am really glad you are here. Over sixteen short lessons we will learn how to write a clear, strong essay, one step at a time. Our guide is the book How to Write Essays by Don Shiach, a former examiner. Everything you hear in this course comes from his advice, told in simple words.\n\nLet us start with a surprise. Two students can know the same amount about a subject and still get very different marks. Why? Because, as the book says, it is not just what you know, but how you apply that knowledge that finally counts.\n\nThe first and biggest rule is this. Answer the exact question you have been given. Not the question you wish they had asked. Not everything you know about the subject. Just the question on the paper. The book puts it in three words. Relevance is all.\n\nPicture a student who is asked about the origins of the First World War. He knows a lot about the battles, the trenches and the peace treaty, so he writes all of it down. It may be a fine piece of writing, but most of it does not answer the question. The question only asked why the war began. A good student picks only the causes, and leaves the rest out, even when it hurts.\n\nSo before you write a single word, slow down. Read the title carefully, twice. Then ask yourself one simple question. What exactly am I being asked to do here?\n\nThe examiner sets the task, not you. Give the examiner what they asked for, and you are already ahead of many students in the room.\n\nHere is how this course works. Listen to each lesson, then look at the worked example on the card. You will see a weak version and a better version side by side. After that, try the short Your turn task. If you like, you can ask the AI coach to check your answer.\n\nWhen this audio ends, the lesson is marked done and the next one opens for you. Let us begin."
  },
  "apostrophes-and-spelling": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/apostrophes-and-spelling.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson fourteen. Apostrophes and spelling.\n\nSome small errors annoy examiners more than you would expect. The apostrophe is near the top of the list.\n\nBegin with it's and its. The one with an apostrophe means it is. The one without means belonging to it. The book shows two newspaper headlines that get it wrong. A headline that shouts its a record needs an apostrophe, because it means it is a record. A headline about a car that says it's wheels came off should not have one, because the wheels belong to it. The book calls mixing these up a bad error.\n\nApostrophes have two main jobs. They show that something belongs to someone, as in my daughter's education or the school's reputation. And they show that letters are missing, as in don't, can't and won't.\n\nPossessive words such as yours, ours, hers and theirs never take an apostrophe.\n\nHere is another common mistake. Never use an apostrophe just to make a plural. Wild animals deserve to be protected from poachers. No apostrophes there at all, because nothing belongs to anything. Only when a plural owns something does it take one, as in politicians' instincts or women's rights.\n\nNow spelling. The book picks out some mix ups that make a poor impression. Their means belonging to them, as in their house. There shows that something exists, or means in that place, as in there is no doubt, or over there. They're is short for they are.\n\nThe same goes for were, where and we're. Were is a part of the verb to be. Where asks about or points to a place. We're is short for we are.\n\nThe book also gives a long list of words you will use often in essays, such as necessary, argument, government and environment. Learn to spell them. And in coursework there is even less excuse, because spelling checkers are always at hand.\n\nYour turn. You will see a short passage full of apostrophe and spelling mistakes. Rewrite it correctly."
  },
  "avoid-waffle": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/avoid-waffle.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson four. Start strong and avoid waffle.\n\nThink about picking up a book in a shop. You read the first paragraph, and that paragraph decides whether you keep reading. Your examiner has to read your essay anyway, but your first paragraph still shapes how they feel about everything that follows. You want a happy reader, not a tired one.\n\nThe biggest enemy of a good opening is waffle. Waffle is empty, general talk that could fit almost any essay. Students use it to hide the fact that they have nothing much to say yet.\n\nHere is the kind of opening the book warns against. This is a very important issue and there are many different approaches that can be taken. Many experts have considered this matter. There are arguments for and against, and many people feel very strongly about it.\n\nWhat is the topic? You cannot tell. The book points out that a paragraph like that says nothing in forty five words. Another favourite is the line that a question has perplexed historians down through the years. It sounds serious, but again it says nothing at all.\n\nSo what should you do instead? Deal with the topic from the very first sentence. A useful trick is to use some of the key words of the title in your opening line.\n\nThe book shows this with the novel Great Expectations. The question says that Pip loses his moral values and has to win them back. A strong first sentence names Pip, names his values, such as kindness and hard work, and says where he lost them, when he left for London. In one sentence the examiner knows you are answering the real question.\n\nBe specific, but do not try to pack everything in. The detail belongs in the body. The opening simply shows that you are on the ball from the very first line.\n\nYour turn. You will see a waffly first sentence for a title about celebrity culture. Rewrite it as one specific sentence that uses the key words of the title."
  },
  "back-it-up": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/back-it-up.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson nine. Back up every claim.\n\nA statement without evidence is only an assertion. The book says that whatever the subject, assertions must be matched with detail and specific references.\n\nIn a literature essay this evidence is called a close reference. It might be a key event in the story, something a character says or does, or a short quotation. The book compares this to a lawyer in court. A good lawyer does not just say that someone is guilty. The lawyer shows the evidence.\n\nHere is the book's example from the novel Pride and Prejudice. The key sentence says that Elizabeth has plenty of reason to think Darcy is proud. Then come three brief references. He refuses to dance with her at the ball. He looks down on her mother. And his marriage proposal suggests he had to overcome his doubts about her lower social position. Three short pieces of evidence, and the claim stands up.\n\nNotice what the writer did not do. They did not retell the whole story. The book is very clear about this. Close references are not the same as telling the story in your own words. Pick a few sharp details that prove your point, and move on.\n\nThe same rule works for history and politics. In an essay on why the Labour Party won the British election of nineteen forty five, the writer does not just say the nineteen thirties were hard. They name mass unemployment, poor housing, hunger and the lack of free medical care. That is real evidence.\n\nQuotations should be short and relevant. And use the proper terms of your subject, without showing off with jargon for its own sake.\n\nFor your ISSB essay this means real names, places, dates and examples, instead of vague words like many people say or some experts believe.\n\nYour turn. You will see a bare claim. Rewrite it as a short paragraph that backs it up with at least two specific pieces of evidence, without telling a long story."
  },
  "closing-paragraph": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/closing-paragraph.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson ten. End with a strong conclusion.\n\nYour opening makes the first impression. Your closing paragraph makes the last one, just before the examiner decides your mark. So it matters just as much.\n\nWhat does a closing paragraph do? It rounds off the essay. If the title asked for a judgement, this is where you give it clearly and sum up your reasons. It brings the essay back to the question that was set.\n\nThere is a trap here. Do not simply repeat every point word for word. The book asks you to find a fresh and concise way to restate where you have arrived. And just like the opening, the ending must avoid waffle.\n\nSignal that you are concluding. The book suggests phrases such as In conclusion, then. Finally. As I have argued. As I have shown. Therefore. Based on this evidence. And the bulk of the evidence, then, points to.\n\nHere is the book's example about saving the planet from ecological disaster. It begins, In conclusion, then, I would argue that unless all the countries of the world make some sacrifices, the resources we have taken for granted will run out. It says the most powerful nations must lead the way. It says the time for empty talk is past and the time for action has arrived. And it ends with one powerful sentence. It is the human race itself that faces extinction.\n\nThat last line is what the book calls a significant final sentence. It is relevant, it sounds final, and it leaves the reader with something to think about. Try to end every essay with a sentence like that.\n\nCompare it with a weak ending, such as, so these were some points about the topic and everyone has their own opinion. It gives no judgement, no summary and no final thought.\n\nYour turn. Write a closing paragraph for the voting age essay. Signal that you are concluding, give your judgement, sum up your reasons in fresh words, and end with a strong final sentence."
  },
  "develop-and-close": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/develop-and-close.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson seven. Develop the point and close the paragraph.\n\nA key sentence on its own is not enough. You must give it flesh, as the book says. That means examples, details and explanation.\n\nThe book shows a neat shape for a body paragraph. First the key sentence. Then a few sentences that develop it with specific examples. Then a closing sentence that rounds the paragraph off.\n\nLet us look at the celebrity paragraph again. After the key sentence, the next line gives an example. Programmes about celebrities appear daily on television. Then come more details, about celebrities sent off to jungles and set strange tests for our entertainment, and producers chasing higher viewing figures.\n\nThen comes the close. It begins with the word Thus. Thus, we the viewers play our part, because we give the producers the audience they need. The book calls this an intermediate conclusion. It is a small conclusion drawn from the evidence in that one paragraph.\n\nThe very last sentence can also point the way to the next paragraph. If we did not watch these programmes, they would soon stop being made. Now the next paragraph can pick up the idea of audience figures and carry it forward.\n\nAnother example in the book is about charity concerts for famine relief. The key sentence says that perhaps nothing has really changed since the first concert. The next sentences give the evidence. Famine, drought, disease and civil war remain. The closing sentence says that many aid workers now look to other solutions, which leads neatly into the next paragraph.\n\nSo remember the pattern. Key sentence. Development with real examples. Closing sentence that sums up or looks ahead.\n\nYour turn. Write one full body paragraph. Start with a key sentence, add three sentences that develop it with specific examples, and finish with a closing sentence. You can use the internet title or the voting age title."
  },
  "exam-technique": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/exam-technique.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson fifteen. Writing under exam conditions.\n\nThe book has a striking idea. When you sit an exam, one of the things being tested is how good you are at sitting exams. Two students with the same knowledge can walk out with very different marks. The difference is how each one meets the demands of the exam.\n\nThe first golden rule is give the examiners what they want. Do not have a mental argument with the paper. Thoughts like this question is unfair will not help you. Just carry out the instructions to the letter.\n\nRead the instructions with great care. If the paper says answer two questions from section A and one from section B, then do exactly that. Answering two from section B because you feel more confident there will simply cost you marks.\n\nNext, divide your time sensibly. Look at the marks for each question. If one part is worth eight marks and another is worth fifteen, spend nearly twice as long on the second.\n\nThe book gives a clear example with four questions worth the same marks. One student spends most of the time on two favourite questions, then rushes the last two. Eighteen, eighteen, twelve and seven. That is fifty five. Another shares the time equally. Seventeen, seventeen, fifteen and fifteen. That is sixty four. Same knowledge, nine extra marks, just from managing time.\n\nWhat if you run out of time and scribble the last answer in note form? The book warns that such answers are usually marked out of only half the marks. Notes are no replacement for a proper essay.\n\nFor your ISSB essay you get about thirty minutes for one essay. So use the method from this course. A few minutes to underline key words and plan. Most of your time for the body. And a couple of minutes at the end for a strong conclusion and a quick check of spelling and punctuation.\n\nYour turn. Write a simple time plan for a thirty minute essay. Say how many minutes you will give to planning, the opening, the body, the conclusion and checking."
  },
  "for-and-against": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/for-and-against.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson eleven. The for and against essay.\n\nLet us pull the structure together. The book sums it up in one line. Your essay needs a beginning, a middle and an end. In that order.\n\nMany essay titles ask you to discuss a statement, weigh up the arguments, or make your own point of view clear. The book calls these discursive essays. You put forward the arguments for and against a point of view, and then you reach a judgement.\n\nThe examiner is checking a few things. Are you clear and concise? Are your points well organised? Do you balance the arguments for and against? And does the essay have a sound overall structure?\n\nThe book warns that these essays can easily ramble. So keep a tight hold on your argument. Back up each point with detailed examples. And do not ignore the other side. Bring in the counter arguments and deal with them.\n\nThe book's sample essay discusses whether watching professional sport has become far too important for many people. Look at how its paragraphs open. However, it is not only the television viewers who are the fanatics. On the other hand, such an obsession may come from a lack of close relationships. Then a paragraph on the role of the media, linked by the words this obsession. Then, it can be argued, then. Then Nevertheless. And finally, In conclusion, then, I would argue.\n\nSee how the essay moves from one side to the other, yet every paragraph is linked to the last. And the conclusion leaves no doubt about the writer's own view.\n\nThe sample also uses a couple of rhetorical questions. These are questions you ask so that you can go on to answer them. The book says they are useful, but do not overdo them.\n\nSo a weak discursive essay rambles from point to point and never says what the writer thinks. A better one weighs each side with examples, deals with the strongest counter argument, and ends with a clear judgement.\n\nYour turn. Take the book's title about banning smoking in all public places. List two arguments for and two against, the counter argument you would answer, and your own final view in one sentence."
  },
  "key-sentence": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/key-sentence.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson six. One point per paragraph, with a key sentence.\n\nYou made a promise in your opening. Now you have to come through with the goods. The body of the essay is where you earn most of your marks.\n\nParagraphs are your best friends here. Imagine a page with no breaks at all. It is tiring to read, and the ideas seem jumbled. Paragraphs guide your reader from one idea to the next in a clear order.\n\nThe book gives a simple rule. Deal with one main point in each paragraph. If you pack three or four points into one paragraph, your reader gets confused, and you end up treating each point only on the surface.\n\nHow do you show the reader what the point is? With a key sentence. The book says it gives the reader a key to unlock what the paragraph is about. Put it first, and your reader knows at once where you are going.\n\nHere is the book's example. A paragraph about celebrity culture begins with this key sentence. Celebrity culture, then, is a well established feature of our mass media. Everything else in the paragraph supports that one idea. Programmes about celebrities appear on television every day. Producers have learnt that the word celebrity brings in more viewers.\n\nNow picture a weak paragraph on the same topic. It talks about celebrities on television, then jumps to fashion, then to the internet, then to young people and their dreams, all in a few lines. There is no key sentence, and no point is developed. The reader is left asking, what is this paragraph really about?\n\nThe book says the more signals like this you give, the more your essay hangs together. One point. One key sentence. Placed at the start.\n\nYour turn. Take the title about the internet being a very mixed blessing. Choose one main point for a body paragraph and write a key sentence for it. Then list the details you would use to support it."
  },
  "learn-from-models": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/learn-from-models.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson twelve. Learn from model essays.\n\nThe second half of the book is full of sample essays. They cover literature, poetry, media, history, novels, film, politics and critical thinking. The big lesson from all of them is simple. The same structure works in every subject. A clear opening, a structured development and a considered conclusion.\n\nEach subject does add its own demands. History and politics need the facts at your fingertips, chosen with care. Literature needs close references and short quotations. Film needs you to describe scenes, because you cannot quote a picture. And every subject has its own proper terms, which you should use naturally.\n\nThe book also gives a checklist of questions an assessor asks while marking. Learn them, because you can ask them of your own essay. Does the opening deal with the topic and say something specific? Does it avoid empty waffle? Does each body paragraph have a key sentence? Does each deal mainly with one key point? Is that point backed up with evidence? Does the last sentence of each paragraph sum up or point ahead? Are the paragraphs linked, with a sense of flow? Does the final paragraph show that a conclusion is being reached, sum up the case, and bring the essay back to the question?\n\nOne chapter teaches critical thinking. Here you read someone else's argument and take it apart. You find the conclusion, the reasons given, and any counter arguments. Then you look for flaws in the reasoning, and for assumptions, things the writer takes for granted without proving them. This habit will help you think for yourself, which matters a great deal at the ISSB.\n\nSo when you read a good essay, do not just read it. Study it. Ask where the key sentences are, which linking words are used, what evidence is given, and how the conclusion is built.\n\nYour turn. You will see a short sample paragraph. Name its key sentence, its linking word, the evidence it gives, and what its closing sentence does."
  },
  "linking-words": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/linking-words.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson eight. Link your paragraphs.\n\nYour reader must be able to follow the flow of your essay. The book wants every essay to feel like one connected whole, with each section fitting neatly into the one before.\n\nThe tool for this is linking words and phrases. They are signposts. They tell your reader where your argument is going, and they show the examiner that you are in control.\n\nHere are some that the book suggests. However. Nevertheless. Furthermore. Moreover. On the contrary. Secondly. Therefore. Thus. Finally. And longer ones, such as another essential feature of, or to counter this argument, or the bulk of the available evidence, then, points to.\n\nThe book shows them at work in an essay on climate change. The first paragraph says that experts may disagree on the causes, but hardly anyone denies that the weather is changing. The second paragraph opens with However, and explains how selfish national interests get in the way. The third opens with Nevertheless, and turns to the progress that has been made. Three small words, and the reader never gets lost.\n\nLinking words do not always have to come first. In one example about sport, the word however is the eighth word of the paragraph. That is fine, as long as it sits in the first sentence. Notice too that when however comes in the middle of a sentence, it needs a comma before it and after it.\n\nSmall words like this and that can also link ideas. A paragraph that talks about this obsession is pointing back to the paragraph before it.\n\nNow picture an essay with no links at all. It jumps from point to point, and the examiner has to work out the connections alone. The book says that is exactly what examiners do not want to read.\n\nYour turn. You will see the first sentences of three body paragraphs with no links. Rewrite each one so that it carries a suitable linking word or phrase."
  },
  "make-a-plan": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/make-a-plan.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson three. Make a plan before you write.\n\nMany students feel that planning wastes time, especially in an exam. The book disagrees strongly. A little thought, turned into brief notes, pays off in relevance, order and clarity.\n\nThink about everyday life. When a friend asks your opinion, your answer sounds much better when you think for a moment before you jump in with both feet. Essays work the same way.\n\nA plan helps two people. It helps you, because a clear shape in your head makes the writing easier. And it helps your reader, the teacher or examiner. The book says meet the assessors more than halfway. Make their job easy, and they will be on your side.\n\nEvery essay has three parts. A definite opening, a considered development, and an emphatic conclusion. The middle part, the body, is by far the longest. But the opening and the ending matter just as much.\n\nSo how do you plan? First, read the title and underline the key words. Then jot down quick words and phrases that come to mind. They can be messy and random at first. Next, keep only what is relevant to the question. Finally, put what is left in order. What goes in the opening? Which points make the body, one for each paragraph? And what will you say at the end?\n\nHow long should this take? The book says no more than five to seven minutes when you have an hour or less for the essay. With a shorter essay, keep it shorter still. Do not get so carried away with notes that you run out of time to write.\n\nAnd remember this. An exam is not a race to fill the most pages. As an examiner, the author sometimes had to put a line through page after page of answers that were simply not relevant. Quality beats length every time.\n\nYour turn. Take the book's title about the internet being a very mixed blessing, and write a short plan. One line for the opening, three or four points for the body, and one line for the conclusion."
  },
  "opening-paragraph": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/opening-paragraph.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson five. Build your opening paragraph.\n\nYou have a strong first sentence. What comes next? The book gives a clear guide. Aim for an opening paragraph of four or five sentences.\n\nThe job of this paragraph is not to answer the whole question. Its job is to map out the ground. You tell the reader what you are going to do. Then in the body you keep your word. And then you round it all off in the conclusion.\n\nLet us walk through an example from the book. The title asks whether the voting age should be lowered to sixteen, and asks you to weigh up the arguments for and against.\n\nSentence one goes straight to the topic. Debate about the voting age usually centres on whether young people are mature enough to make a reasoned judgement about their vote.\n\nSentence two follows on. It asks whether most voters of any age really vote with mature judgement, or simply out of habit or prejudice.\n\nSentence three pushes the idea further. Perhaps maturity is not the real issue at all.\n\nSentence four opens the next door. More central to the debate may be the rights of every citizen in a democracy, whatever their age.\n\nNotice what happened. Each sentence grows out of the one before it. And each one is a small promise. Maturity, the habits of voters, and the rights of citizens. The reader now expects all three to come back later in the essay.\n\nThe book also offers some useful starting phrases. In this essay I intend to explore. This essay will discuss. This essay will focus on. You do not have to use them, but they give a brisk and direct start.\n\nBefore you move on, test your opening with the book's own questions. Does it deal with the topic at once? Is it specific? Does it map out what comes next? Does it avoid waffle?\n\nYour turn. Write an opening paragraph of four or five sentences for the book's title about the warming of the planet being the most serious issue that faces mankind today."
  },
  "put-it-together": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/put-it-together.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson sixteen. Put it all together.\n\nWell done. You have reached the final lesson. Let us gather everything into one simple routine that you can use in the ISSB essay, or anywhere else.\n\nStep one. Read the title twice and underline the key words. Ask what exactly you are being asked to do. Remember, relevance is all.\n\nStep two. Spend a few minutes on a plan. Jot down ideas, keep only what is relevant, and put the rest in order. One main point for each body paragraph.\n\nStep three. Write an opening of four or five sentences. Deal with the topic from the first line, use the key words, avoid waffle, and map out the ground you will cover.\n\nStep four. Write the body. Each paragraph has one main point, opened by a key sentence. Develop it with specific examples and evidence, not vague claims and not a retold story. Close each paragraph with a sentence that sums up or looks ahead. Link your paragraphs with words like however, furthermore and nevertheless.\n\nStep five. If the title asks you to discuss, weigh up both sides, deal with the counter arguments, and make your own view clear.\n\nStep six. Write a closing paragraph. Signal it, give your judgement, sum up in fresh words, and end with a significant final sentence.\n\nStep seven. Keep a few minutes to check. Complete sentences, sensible commas, correct apostrophes and careful spelling.\n\nAnd above all, remember the book's own summary. Your essay needs a beginning, a middle and an end. In that order.\n\nYou are ready. When this lesson ends, press the button to take the full Essay test. Pick a title, plan, write, and let the AI examiner mark your work against these same rules. Then read the feedback, fix one thing, and write again. That is how good writers are made. Good luck."
  },
  "sentences-and-commas": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/sentences-and-commas.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson thirteen. Sentences and commas.\n\nA well planned essay can still lose marks through careless mistakes. The book admits that anyone can make an error, especially when writing fast. The trick is to keep them few. An occasional slip can be overlooked, but a page full of them will cost you.\n\nStart with complete sentences. A complete sentence makes sense on its own. The country declared war on the day after the invasion. That is complete. Because the voters turned against the party. That is not. It leaves you waiting for the rest. If you are unsure, read the sentence in your head and ask whether it makes sense alone.\n\nNext, mix your sentence lengths. A short, simple sentence makes an emphatic point. Africa is facing a famine crisis. A longer, complex sentence lets you explain and analyse in detail. Good essays use both.\n\nWhat you should avoid is a long chain of short sentences. The book shows a paragraph about fashion models made of seven short sentences. Models only parade on a catwalk. They wear designer clothes. Yet they earn huge sums of money. It sounds choppy and monotonous. Joined into three sentences, with words like because and which, it flows and sounds more thoughtful.\n\nNow full stops. Some students join whole sentences with commas. The football authorities are very concerned about agents, they are seen to be profiting from the game. That comma is not strong enough. It needs a full stop, and a capital letter to start the next sentence.\n\nLinking words such as however, of course and nevertheless usually take commas. At the start of a sentence, put a comma after them. In the middle of a sentence, put one before and one after.\n\nFinally, beware of what the book calls comma itis, a rash of commas scattered everywhere. The rock star, reacted angrily, to the questions. Those commas do not belong. Use a comma only where the meaning needs it.\n\nYour turn. You will see a short passage with sentences run together and too many commas. Rewrite it with correct full stops and commas."
  },
  "underline-key-words": {
    "audio": "/audio/essay-course/underline-key-words.mp3",
    "script": "Lesson two. Underline the key words.\n\nYou now know the golden rule. Answer the question that is set. But how do you make sure you really understand the question? The book gives a simple trick. Underline three or four key words in the title before you plan anything.\n\nLet us try it with a question about Shakespeare. Why does Hamlet delay carrying out his revenge for the murder of his father? Now underline four words. Why, Hamlet, delay and revenge.\n\nEach word gives you a job. Why tells you to explain reasons, not to retell the story. Hamlet tells you it is his reasons that matter, not those of another character. Delay tells you the whole essay is about his waiting. And revenge reminds you what he is waiting to do.\n\nHere is a second example. What were the origins of the First World War? Underline origins and First World War. Suddenly your mind is fixed on the events that led up to the war, and nothing else.\n\nA longer question needs a few more words underlined. One media question asks whether the government should step in to stop a few powerful owners from controlling the newspapers, magazines and channels. The book underlines government intervene, different media, owned and controlled, and few media moguls. Every one of those words points to something you must discuss.\n\nWhy does this help? Because it focuses your thinking and stops you from showing off everything you know. The book says it plainly. Pick and choose well.\n\nIn your ISSB essay you will have very little time, so this trick is gold. It takes ten seconds, and it can save your whole essay.\n\nNow try the Your turn task. You will see a title from the book about lowering the voting age to sixteen. Write down the key words you would underline, and say in a few words what each one tells you to do."
  }
};
